{"uid":"7f7d1d21bb151e97","name":"test_a_historical_zero_token_work_record_reads_back_without_the_generic_divergence","fullName":"tests.test_cli_fault_explanation#test_a_historical_zero_token_work_record_reads_back_without_the_generic_divergence","historyId":"400c3d3e826cf9bd8b13ea910c565752","time":{"start":1791880815153,"stop":1791880815158,"duration":5},"description":"Task 021 (requirement G) shipped this node seeding an already-correct\n    `infra` fixture (iteration 5, recorded `faultClass=\"infra\"`) that never\n    exercised a real disagreement at all -- the re-derivation agreed with\n    what was recorded and nothing diverged. Task cure-01-08 (F08) rewrites it\n    in place, keeping that first case and ADDING the one that matters: the\n    shape #213's own reference run recorded BEFORE the fix -- iteration 158\n    of `selfdev-v022-the-harness-owns-its-own-state`, `exitCode=0`,\n    `error=\"terminated\"`, `faultClass=\"work\"`, every usage counter zero.\n\n    Iteration 158 is the ORIGINAL recorded classification, not a fixture\n    pre-corrected to already read `infra`: the OLD engine read the\n    zero-filled `usage` dict's mere presence as traffic (`bool(usage)`),\n    landed on `work`, and wrote that to disk. Today's rule\n    (`billable_tokens(usage) > 0`) re-derives `infra` for the same iteration,\n    so `faultClass` and the re-derivation genuinely disagree -- but that\n    disagreement is fully explained by the fix itself\n    (`state._recorded_fault_notice`: the OLD presence-of-usage question\n    reproduces the recorded verdict exactly), so it is NOT the generic\n    `FAULT_VERDICT_DIVERGED_NOTICE` -- both `iteration_detail` and\n    `fault_explanation` say so the same way.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task 021 (requirement G) shipped this node seeding an already-correct<br />\n<code>infra</code> fixture (iteration 5, recorded <code>faultClass=&quot;infra&quot;</code>) that never<br />\nexercised a real disagreement at all -- the re-derivation agreed with<br />\nwhat was recorded and nothing diverged. Task cure-01-08 (F08) rewrites it<br />\nin place, keeping that first case and ADDING the one that matters: the<br />\nshape #213's own reference run recorded BEFORE the fix -- iteration 158<br />\nof <code>selfdev-v022-the-harness-owns-its-own-state</code>, <code>exitCode=0</code>,<br />\n<code>error=&quot;terminated&quot;</code>, <code>faultClass=&quot;work&quot;</code>, every usage counter zero.</p>\n<pre><code>Iteration 158 is the ORIGINAL recorded classification, not a fixture\npre-corrected to already read `infra`: the OLD engine read the\nzero-filled `usage` dict's mere presence as traffic (`bool(usage)`),\nlanded on `work`, and wrote that to disk. Today's rule\n(`billable_tokens(usage) &gt; 0`) re-derives `infra` for the same iteration,\nso `faultClass` and the re-derivation genuinely disagree -- but that\ndisagreement is fully explained by the fix itself\n(`state._recorded_fault_notice`: the OLD presence-of-usage question\nreproduces the recorded verdict exactly), so it is NOT the generic\n`FAULT_VERDICT_DIVERGED_NOTICE` -- both `iteration_detail` and\n`fault_explanation` say so the same way.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791880795037,"stop":1791880795037,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791880795038,"stop":1791880795038,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791880815141,"stop":1791880815141,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"p